  "body": {
    "type": "doc",
    "content": [
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "This recipe serves 4 as a starter and takes around 30 minutes. Silken tofu is pressed dry, dusted in potato starch or cornflour, and deep-fried until it has a thin, crisp shell. The"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "agedashi"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "technique creates a contrast between the crisp coating and the soft, silky interior. The tofu is served in a warm"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "dashi"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "broth thickened into an"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "ankake"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "sauce that clings to the outside."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Pressing the tofu is not optional. Silken tofu contains a great deal of water. If it goes into the oil wet, it will spit violently and the coating will not adhere. Wrap the block in 3 layers of kitchen paper and set a plate on top for at least 20 minutes. The coating must be cornflour or potato starch only, not flour. Flour creates a thick, doughy shell that softens in the broth. Starch gives a thin, glassy coating that holds its crunch a little longer."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "What you need"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
